---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/base';
---
<Guide
  title="A domain you already own"
  heading="A domain you"
  thin="already own."
  intro="Most people come to me with a web address already bought, which is fine — it just means the settings behind it live in somebody else's account. Pointing it at your new site, setting up your email, or working out why something stopped loading all happen in there, so I need a way in."
  description="If you already own a domain name, here are the ways to let Weaver Digital change its settings — at Porkbun, GoDaddy, Namecheap or anywhere else."
>
  <div class="note">
    <p><strong>Your domain stays yours.</strong> I never need it transferred and I never need to own it. All I need is to be able to change its settings, and only while there is something to change.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="guide-section">
    <h2>When this comes up</h2>
    <p>Usually one of three things:</p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Connecting your site.</strong> A couple of records that point your web address at the site I built you. This is the one everybody needs, once.</li>
      <li><strong>Setting up your email.</strong> If you want mail at your own address, records have to say which mail service handles it.</li>
      <li><strong>Something is not working.</strong> Site not loading, email bouncing. Finding out why means reading those same records.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="guide-section">
    <h2>First, where does your domain live?</h2>
    <p>Whoever you pay each year for the name is the one holding its settings — GoDaddy, Namecheap, Squarespace, Wix, Network Solutions, Porkbun, your old web guy's account. That is the company I need to get into.</p>
    <p><strong>Not sure?</strong> You do not have to go digging. Send me your web address and I can look up who holds it in about a minute. Then tell me which of the three ways below suits you.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="guide-section">
    <h2>Three ways to let me in</h2>
    <p>Any of these works. Pick whichever you are most comfortable with.</p>

    <div class="ways">
      <div class="way card">
        <span class="meta">Works anywhere</span>
        <h3>Get on a short call with me</h3>
        <p>You sign in, share your screen, and I tell you exactly what to click. Fifteen minutes, usually less. Nothing changes hands, you see every change as it is made, and you know afterwards where the settings live.</p>
        <p>This is the one I suggest first, and the only one that works no matter who holds your domain.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="way card">
        <span class="meta">Porkbun, and a few others</span>
        <h3>Add me as a user on your account</h3>
        <p>Some registrars let you give someone else permission to make changes without handing over your password. Porkbun calls it an <strong>authorized user</strong>; GoDaddy calls it <strong>delegate access</strong>. It is the tidiest option where it exists — I can do the work in my own time, and you can remove me whenever you like.</p>
        <p>At Porkbun? <a href="#porkbun">The steps are below</a>. Somewhere else? Tell me who holds your domain and I will tell you whether yours can do this and what to add.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="way card">
        <span class="meta">Works anywhere</span>
        <h3>Send me the login</h3>
        <p>Plenty of clients just do this and it is perfectly fine. Text it to me rather than emailing it if you can, and change the password once I tell you I am finished. I do not keep it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="note warn">
    <p><strong>Tell me first if you already use email on your domain.</strong> Something like you@yourbusiness.com. Your email runs off the same settings as your website, so I will work around it — but only if I know it is there. I would rather be careful than take your email down for an afternoon.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="guide-section" id="porkbun">
    <h2>If your domain is at Porkbun</h2>
    <p>Adding me as an authorized user takes about ten minutes and costs nothing. I get to make changes for you without ever knowing your password — and I cannot transfer your domain or change your password, only its settings.</p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn btn-blue" href={url('/guides/giving-us-access-to-porkbun.pdf')} download>
        Download the walkthrough <span class="arrow">→</span>
      </a>
    </div>
    <p>That walkthrough has a picture of every screen. Open it alongside this page — this is the summary, that is the detail.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="guide-section">
    <h2>Before you start</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Your Porkbun login.</strong> Username and password.</li>
      <li><strong>Your phone, with an authenticator app.</strong> Google Authenticator or Microsoft Authenticator. Both are free.</li>
      <li><strong>Your email.</strong> Porkbun may send a verification code if it has not verified your address yet.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Porkbun asks for all of this because an authorized user can make changes on your account, so it wants both accounts locked down first. Mine already is.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="steps-title">What you will do</h2>

  <div class="steps">
    <div class="step reveal">
      <h2>Verify your phone and email</h2>
      <p>Click <strong>Account</strong> at the top right, then <strong>Settings / Billing</strong>. You land on <strong>Your details</strong>.</p>
      <p>Click <strong>send verification</strong> beside your phone number and type in the code Porkbun texts you. Check your primary email says verified too, and do the same if it does not.</p>
      <p>You can ignore ID verification on that page — it is optional.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="step reveal">
      <h2>Turn on two-factor sign-in</h2>
      <p>Install Google Authenticator or Microsoft Authenticator on your phone first if you do not have one.</p>
      <p>Still in Settings / Billing, click <strong>Security and sign-in</strong>, and switch on <strong>Two-factor authentication with an app</strong>. Scan the QR code with the app, type the six digits back in, and submit.</p>
      <div class="note warn">
        <p><strong>Save the backup codes</strong> Porkbun shows you next. They are what get you in if you lose your phone. Never share them with anyone — me included.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step reveal">
      <h2>Add Weaver Digital</h2>
      <p>Click <strong>Account</strong> again and choose <strong>Authorized Users</strong>, near the bottom of a fairly long menu — just below Subaccounts.</p>
      <p>Under <strong>Add Authorized User</strong>, type <code>weaverdigital</code> as the username and click <strong>Submit</strong>.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="step reveal">
      <h2>Let me know</h2>
      <p>Email or text me once you have hit Submit. I get the invitation, accept it, and you will see me listed on your Authorized Users page.</p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="mailto:ashley@weaverdigital.io?subject=Access%20to%20my%20domain">Email ashley@weaverdigital.io <span class="arrow">→</span></a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="guide-section">
    <h2>Removing me later</h2>
    <p>At Porkbun, go to <strong>Account → Authorized Users</strong> and revoke the access. It takes effect straight away, and you do not have to tell me first.</p>
    <p>Anywhere else, remove the delegate access or change the password — same thing, and no hard feelings either way.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="guide-section">
    <h2>Do not have a domain yet?</h2>
    <p>Then this is not your guide. Start with <a href={url('/guides/your-web-address')}>getting your web address</a> — you buy the name yourself, in your name, and it takes about fifteen minutes.</p>
  </div>

  <style>
    .steps-title { font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.1rem); margin: 56px 0 18px; }
    ul.plain { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 16px 0 20px; display: grid; gap: 12px; }
    ul.plain li { color: var(--gray); }
    ul.plain strong { color: var(--charcoal); }
    .ways { display: grid; gap: 14px; margin-top: 22px; }
    .way { padding: 28px 32px; }
    .way .meta {
      display: inline-block; font-size: 0.74rem; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: 0.06em;
      text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--gray); margin-bottom: 8px;
    }
    .way h3 { font-size: 1.2rem; letter-spacing: -0.02em; line-height: 1.25; margin-bottom: 10px; }
    .way p { color: var(--gray); }
    .way p + p { margin-top: 12px; }
    .way strong { color: var(--charcoal); font-weight: 600; }
    @media (max-width: 700px) {
      .way { padding: 24px; }
    }
  </style>
</Guide>
